1. Least Privilege:
This is extremely important in DevOps.

Least privilege means:

Give only the permissions required to perform the task.

Suppose your application only needs:

S3 → Read objects

Don't give:

AdministratorAccess

Instead give something close to:

s3:GetObject

for only the required bucket/path.

Bad
Application
   ↓
AdministratorAccess
Better
Application
   ↓
Specific IAM Role
   ↓
s3:GetObject
   ↓
Specific bucket



2. Allow vs Deny:
IAM policies contain an effect.

Usually:

Allow
Deny

Example:

{
  "Effect": "Allow",
  "Action": "s3:GetObject",
  "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::saishop-images/*"
}

Means:
Allow reading objects.


3. Explicit Deny:
AWS IAM has an important rule:

An explicit Deny overrides an Allow.

Example:

Policy 1:
Allow s3:DeleteObject

Policy 2:
Deny s3:DeleteObject

Effective result:

DeleteObject
     ↓
     ❌ DENIED

Even though there is an Allow.

Remember:
Explicit Deny
     ↓
Overrides Allow
